#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198154 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b31f2bdc-c267-5fc7-bd0f-283777ee4fee" :knowledge-point/title "The Derivative of a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a5637d71-22d4-548e-ad36-d09bff61d643" :question/math-academy-id "e-2398" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) = \\langle \\sin t,\\cos t, t \\rangle$ at $t= \\dfrac{\\pi}4$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}4\\right)\n$$\n\nwe substitute\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}4\n$$\n\ninto the expression for $\\mathbf f'(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= ⟨\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}),-\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}), 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198157 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "072ad553-dd0b-58e0-b3e2-082c6d9866dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c737d317-8e09-5ccb-b816-b5f56e925ba9" :question/math-academy-id "e-609" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 10t^4,\\, 6\\ln t\\rangle$, what is $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t) = 10t^4$, we have\n$x'(t) = 40t^3$,\nand therefore\n$x'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 40\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = 5$.\n- For the $y$-component $y(t) = 6 \\ln t$, we have\n$y^{′}(t) = \\frac{6}{t}$,\nand therefore\n$y'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{6}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}=12$.\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) &= ⟨x^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}), y^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 12⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198158 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0e2596-73d6-540c-af84-b19a943da6a8" :knowledge-point/title "The Second Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "cde8f304-4937-5eb7-a8e1-a08355ee4d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-611" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t^6,\\, t^5\\rangle$, Calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$." :question/worked-solution "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t) = t^6$, we have\n$x^{′}(t)|= 6t^{5}; x^{″}(t)|= 30t^{4}$.\n- For $y(t) = t^5$, we have\n$y^{′}(t)|= 5t^{4}; y^{″}(t)|= 20t^{3}$.\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨x^{″}(t), y^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨30t^{4}, 20t^{3}⟩ \\\\\n&= 10⟨3t^{4}, 2t^{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
